    "topic": "Phone interviews: Pros and cons",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "exam_date": "17/06/2026 - Real Exam",
    "prompt": "What are the pros and cons of conducting job interviews over the phone?",
    "prompt_vi": "Những thuận lợi và khó khăn khi tiến hành các buổi phỏng vấn xin việc qua điện thoại là gì? Hãy phân tích chi tiết hai mặt của phương thức này kèm ví dụ cụ thể.",
    "has_sample": true,
    "sample_answer": "Nowadays, many companies choose to conduct job interviews over the phone instead of meeting candidates in person. In my opinion, while this method offers convenience and saves costs, it also has some drawbacks, such as a lack of non-verbal communication and potential signal issues.\nOn the one hand, conducting phone interviews brings several clear advantages. First, it saves a lot of time and money for both employers and job seekers. Candidates do not need to spend money on travel or worry about traffic jams, while recruiters can save office space. Second, phone interviews are a very fast tool for initial screening. For instance, when I worked as a clerk at a clothing brand called The ABC, our manager always used phone calls to quickly talk to part-time applicants before inviting the best ones for a formal meeting. This step saved a lot of time and helped filter out unqualified candidates efficiently.\nOn the other hand, there are some notable disadvantages to phone interviews. The main drawback is the lack of body language and facial expressions. Because interviewers cannot see the candidate, it is difficult to judge their confidence, enthusiasm, and overall personality. In addition, technical problems can easily affect the interview. Poor phone signals or sudden background noises can interrupt the conversation and create misunderstandings between both sides.\nIn conclusion, phone interviews provide great benefits in terms of saving time and screening candidates quickly. However, they also make it hard to evaluate applicants fully without face-to-face interaction. Therefore, I believe companies should use phone interviews as a preliminary step rather than a final hiring decision.",
