    "category_vi": "Môi trường công sở",
    "topic": "Employees evaluating their managers",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "exam_date": "15/04/2026 - Real Exam",
    "prompt": "Pros and cons allowing employees to evaluate their managers",
    "prompt_vi": "Những ưu điểm và rủi ro tiềm ẩn khi cho phép nhân viên cấp dưới đánh giá năng lực của các nhà quản lý/cấp trên là gì? Hãy phân tích chi tiết và đưa ra ví dụ minh họa.",
    "has_sample": true,
    "sample_answer": "Evaluating managers is a new trend in many modern workplaces. While this policy has some negative points, I believe that allowing employees to judge their bosses brings many great benefits to a company.\nFirst of all, giving feedback to managers helps improve communication and creates a better working environment. In many traditional offices, managers do not know how their team feels because workers are often afraid to speak up. When employees can share their honest opinions, managers can see their own mistakes and change their leadership style. For example, when I worked as a clerk at a clothing brand called The ABC, our manager allowed us to evaluate her every month. Because of our feedback, she stopped shouting during busy sales seasons. This made us feel safe and brave enough to make mistakes, fix them, and work much harder for the store.\nOn the other hand, this system can sometimes cause bad relationships and reduce work efficiency. If employees use this choice to give angry or unfair comments, it can really hurt their managers' feelings. Some workers might even use negative feedback to attack a boss they do not like personally. This can easily lead to unnecessary arguments and break teamwork. In addition, managers might feel afraid to give difficult tasks to their staff because they worry about getting bad scores from them. Therefore, this policy can sometimes make a team weaker.\nIn conclusion, allowing employees to evaluate their managers has both pros and cons. Although it can cause some arguments or fear, the benefits of building honest communication are much greater. By using this system in a polite way, companies can create a happy workplace where everyone wants to work harder.",
